   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises and their support systems to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the Corona virus pandemic, the national productivity institute of Mauritius, National Productivity and Competitiveness Council (NPCC) strengthened its operational capacity to provide productivity technical support to Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs), which had been hit hard by the pandemic and the subsequent closure of international tourism in Mauritius. 

NPCC &#x2013; as an entity of the Ministry of Finance and Economic Development &#x2013; operationalized the Operation COSHARE programme which sought to promote productivity for SMEs the tourism sector at national level based on the ILO SCORE programme in which NPCC trainers had been certified in 2021. NPCC adopted the SCORE programme as part of its standard productivity programme for SMEs in Mauritius. 

Following the implementation of Operation COSHARE, the Ministry of Environment approached NPCC and asked for their assistance in operationalising the programme for SMEs in Mauritius targeted by the ministry of environment to ensure SMEs are increasingly following sustainability principle of resource efficiency and cleaner production (RECP). The NPCC, in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment and the Human Resource Development Council, is now implementing the SCORE4CLIMATE programme in additional SMEs, which has the RECP focus. 

Lastly, following a cabinet decision in early 2023, Operation COSHARE will now be replicated annually and implemented by NPCC as the national productivity organisation. During 2022 the ILO collaborated closely with NPCC and provided technical assistance to a) adapt the SCORE module on OSH, which became the basis for the Operation COSHARE programme b) train NPCC SCORE trainers in the adapted SCORE module, c) train NPCC SCORE trainers in the new SCORE4CLIMATE Module and d) certify new NPCC SCORE trainers, e) share the lessons learned from the programme in a national knowledge sharing event on 20-22 September 2022. 

Operation COSHARE was based on the ILO Sustainable and Responsible Enterprises package (SCORE), which seeks to improve the productivity and competitiveness of SMEs through a focus on improved workplace collaboration, quality management, resource efficiency and cleaner production and not least OSH and good human resource management. 

Operation COSHARE had a double goal. One goal was to make tourism enterprises COVID-19 safe for the return of tourists through the implementation of safety protocols and procedures. A second goal was to make enterprises more resource efficient to ensure that economic recovery would not be at the expense of sustainable development and the pristine environment in Mauritius.

Funding was made available under UN Partnership for Action on Green Economy (PAGE) under the Green Economic Recovery support to Mauritius. As a PAGE agency, the ILO received USD50,000 for this programme. 

Specific outputs are a) 12 ILO Certified NPCC trainers, b) training packages on Operation COSHARE (adapted SCORE Module on OSH) and SCORE4CLIMATE, c) 22 cases studies of how enterprises applied the new productivity and RECP knowledge, d) two videos documenting the experience, which has been shared widely on national media and on national TV and e) more than 1,200 SMEs &#x2013; including taxi and tour operators in the tourism sector&#x2013; trained in Operation COSHARE. The international airport of Mauritius also participated in and was trained on Operation COSHARE.</narrative>
   </description>
